-* Reminders (when the lead owner feature is on): a lead is reminded once after the wait if its step is still before `reminder_until`, even if its follow-up email has not been sent yet, because the reminder asks whether the lead moved on, not whether the email was sent. A lead created while the feature was off has no owner, step or reminder, and the step route answers `no_state` for it; only leads created after the switch are covered. A reminder is not sent a second time: one that failed or could not be confirmed is listed for a person (`tests/test_reminder_email.py` runs ten passes and ten racing passes and checks one reminder each).
+* Reminders (when the lead owner feature is on): a lead is reminded once after the wait if its step is still before `reminder_until`, even if its follow-up email has not been sent yet, because the reminder asks whether the lead moved on, not whether the email was sent. A lead created while the feature was off has no owner, step or reminder, and the step route answers `no_state` for it; only leads created after the switch are covered. A reminder is not sent a second time: one that failed or could not be confirmed is listed for a person (`tests/test_reminder_email.py` checks one reminder over many passes, and that ten racing claims give one winner).
 * A person answering "not sent" wrongly for an UNKNOWN follow-up on a mail system that cannot recognise repeats (then a second email can go).
   UNKNOWN items stay unseen unless someone opens the review list.
 * A follow-up stuck in `sending` (for example because the database stayed locked through all retries) is put back by the desk's background job (the "reaper", started by `main`, one pass every second) when it has been stuck for more than 30 seconds by the computer's calendar clock (a clock step of 30 s or more forward can put back a follow-up that is still being sent: on a keyed mail system it is sent again with the same key, on a blind one it becomes `unknown`; after a step backward the reaper sets the stamp of a pending or sending row that lies in the future to the new time, so such a row ages normally and is put back 30 s later; `_restamp_future` in `src/lead_desk/desk.py`, `tests/test_clock_steps.py`): to `pending` on a keyed mail system, to `unknown` on a blind one, when the send may have started.
