 **Status of the scan:** the first live run of this workflow on GitHub, as read from the job log by the project lead (not by the author of this note): run 43403502735, 2026-11-13, head 15b8b15e. The log says "Scanning 64 files tracked by git with 79 Code rules" and "Ran 77 rules on 47 files: 0 findings", and the job ended with exit code 0 (Semgrep 1.185.0, `p/python` and `p/javascript`, severity ERROR, `--strict`, the five folders above). Why the numbers differ (79 rules and 64 files in the first line, 77 rules and 47 files in the second) is not explained here. **A green job alone does not prove that rules ran:** a job can end green with no rules loaded or no files read. The proof is the log line "Ran N rules on M files": after any change to the packs or the folders, read it and check that N and M are not zero and are plausible. This workflow has no check of its own for those numbers. `tests/` and `demo_only/` are not scanned, and findings of lower severity were not looked at. The rules are fetched live, so a later run can give other numbers. An earlier run by the lead, on commit 0841f36 of a branch that is not in main's history (in the `semgrep/semgrep:1.185.0` container image, without `--strict`, with `--quiet`), also had no ERROR finding (as reported by the lead; no log is shipped); the live run above replaces it as the status. If a run fails on a finding that already exists, fix the code or suppress that one finding as described below in a separate change.
 
-**Licence and the public log:** the rules are said to be under the Semgrep Rules License v1.0 (named from memory of the Semgrep website; the licence text was not fetched or read for this check, so its terms are not stated or checked here). The repository owner's permission to use the registry packs `p/python` and `p/javascript` is not recorded in this repository; the workflow loads only those two packs. The job log of a public repository prints the rule ids and rule messages of every finding, so anyone can read them; the files in this repository contain no copy of any rule.
+**Licence and the public log:** the licence terms of the rule packs were not read or checked for this repository; the repository owner agreed to use them here (the workflow loads only the two packs `p/python` and `p/javascript`). The job log of a public repository prints the rule ids and rule messages of every finding, so anyone can read them; the files in this repository contain no copy of any rule.
 
 **What is scanned, and what is not:** `tests/` is left out on purpose: it holds planted fake secrets, hostile inputs and deliberately insecure code that exist to be caught by other checks, and Semgrep would report them. `demo_only/` (fake services and a demo page) is left out because it is not code the desk runs for real. This is a decision made without a scan result; add either folder to the command in the workflow and in the line below if you want them read. `n8n` holds 3 JSON files (the workflows) and is only nominal: Semgrep is not expected to review the n8n workflows (a JSON file holds the JavaScript of a Code node only as text inside a string); this was not tested here. A comment cannot be put in JSON either, so there is nothing to suppress there.
 
